1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,161
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:04,161 --> 00:00:09,389
This is chapter forty-one, the first of the testing section: JUnit 5.

3
00:00:09,839 --> 00:00:14,439
Pure logic with no dependencies is the easiest code there is to test.

4
00:00:14,439 --> 00:00:25,839
And yet it's often checked with a main method and a human comparing printed numbers, which doesn't run in CI and doesn't tell you which of eleven pricing rules just broke.

5
00:00:26,739 --> 00:00:32,073
Here's a cart total calculator: line totals, then a chain of pricing rules.

6
00:00:32,073 --> 00:00:36,553
Pure logic, exactly the code that should be trivially testable.

7
00:00:37,003 --> 00:00:40,994
A test framework gives you three things a main method can't.

8
00:00:40,994 --> 00:00:44,253
A standard way to declare what should be checked.

9
00:00:44,253 --> 00:00:47,446
Isolation, so one failure doesn't hide the next.

10
00:00:47,446 --> 00:00:51,104
And a machine-readable result that a build can fail on.

11
00:00:52,004 --> 00:00:55,793
JUnit 5 is three components released together.

12
00:00:55,793 --> 00:00:59,253
The Platform discovers and launches tests.

13
00:00:59,253 --> 00:01:02,713
It's the API build tools and IDEs talk to.

14
00:01:03,163 --> 00:01:07,153
Jupiter is the programming model you actually write against.

15
00:01:07,603 --> 00:01:12,595
And Vintage runs your existing JUnit 4 tests on the same platform.

16
00:01:12,595 --> 00:01:16,755
So you can migrate gradually, with no big-bang cutover.

17
00:01:17,205 --> 00:01:19,745
The other big change is extensions.

18
00:01:19,745 --> 00:01:26,278
JUnit 4's RunWith gave you exactly one runner, so Mockito's and Spring's couldn't coexist.

19
00:01:26,278 --> 00:01:33,610
ExtendWith composes, which is why Mockito, Spring and Testcontainers all work in the same test class.

20
00:01:34,510 --> 00:01:36,979
Here's a test that fails usefully.

21
00:01:36,979 --> 00:01:38,577
Arrange an empty cart.

22
00:01:38,577 --> 00:01:41,627
Act, by calling total inside assertThrows.

23
00:01:41,627 --> 00:01:44,387
And assert on the exception's message.

24
00:01:44,837 --> 00:01:47,002
Three things are doing real work.

25
00:01:47,002 --> 00:01:56,254
The name, method, scenario, expected result, means that when CI reports total empty cart throws, you know what broke before opening anything.

26
00:01:56,254 --> 00:02:02,093
assertThrows returns the exception, so you can check you triggered the path you meant to.

27
00:02:02,093 --> 00:02:05,767
Otherwise a NullPointerException from a typo would pass.

28
00:02:05,767 --> 00:02:13,509
And JUnit creates a new instance of the class for every test, so a field mutated in one test can't leak into the next.

29
00:02:14,409 --> 00:02:18,839
When one behaviour has several observable parts, use assertAll.

30
00:02:18,839 --> 00:02:22,495
Every assertion runs, and every failure is reported.

31
00:02:22,945 --> 00:02:28,003
Without it, the first failing assertion stops the method and hides the rest.

32
00:02:28,003 --> 00:02:35,190
Knowing that both the status and the lines are wrong is usually a different diagnosis from either one alone.

33
00:02:36,090 --> 00:02:39,379
Parameterized tests turn one test into many.

34
00:02:39,379 --> 00:02:45,583
Each CSV row is an independent test with its own result, named from the parameters.

35
00:02:46,033 --> 00:02:51,583
The boundary values, nine and ten, forty-nine and fifty, are the entire point.

36
00:02:51,583 --> 00:02:53,504
Off-by-one bugs live there.

37
00:02:53,504 --> 00:03:00,263
A loop inside one test would report a single failure and leave you hunting for which iteration.

38
00:03:00,713 --> 00:03:08,713
When the arguments are objects rather than literals, use MethodSource, with a static method returning a stream of arguments.

39
00:03:09,613 --> 00:03:12,694
Nested classes group tests by precondition.

40
00:03:12,694 --> 00:03:15,775
The outer setup creates ten units of stock.

41
00:03:15,775 --> 00:03:20,146
The inner class, when stock is insufficient, drains it first.

42
00:03:20,146 --> 00:03:25,591
Lifecycle methods run outside in: the outer before-each, then the inner one.

43
00:03:26,041 --> 00:03:29,021
And the report reads like a specification.

44
00:03:29,021 --> 00:03:32,568
When stock is available, it reserves the quantity.

45
00:03:32,568 --> 00:03:37,817
When stock is insufficient, it refuses, and leaves the quantity unchanged.

46
00:03:38,717 --> 00:03:39,553
A few traps.

47
00:03:39,553 --> 00:03:42,897
Disabled means the test is broken or irrelevant.

48
00:03:42,897 --> 00:03:50,142
An assumption means the test is fine, but its preconditions aren't met here, like a missing sandbox URL.

49
00:03:50,142 --> 00:03:55,507
It's reported as skipped, not passed, so it doesn't inflate your green count.

50
00:03:55,957 --> 00:04:00,635
assertTimeoutPreemptively runs the code on a different thread.

51
00:04:00,635 --> 00:04:08,030
So thread-local state, like a Spring transaction, security context, or logging MDC, isn't visible.

52
00:04:08,030 --> 00:04:12,255
Prefer plain assertTimeout unless you must abort a hang.

53
00:04:12,705 --> 00:04:14,293
And parallel execution.

54
00:04:14,293 --> 00:04:20,574
The conservative setting runs classes concurrently and methods within a class sequentially.

55
00:04:20,574 --> 00:04:24,715
Turning it on surfaces every hidden dependency in the suite.

56
00:04:24,715 --> 00:04:27,959
A good audit, but not the day before a release.

57
00:04:28,859 --> 00:04:30,213
Production rules.

58
00:04:30,213 --> 00:04:33,796
Name tests method, scenario, expected result.

59
00:04:33,796 --> 00:04:36,026
Follow arrange, act, assert.

60
00:04:36,026 --> 00:04:38,256
Parameterize the boundaries.

61
00:04:38,256 --> 00:04:41,601
Group with nested classes by precondition.

62
00:04:41,601 --> 00:04:43,910
Assert on exception messages.

63
00:04:43,910 --> 00:04:46,459
Use the BOM, and tag slow tests.

64
00:04:46,909 --> 00:04:48,836
Never depend on test order.

65
00:04:48,836 --> 00:04:54,547
Never write assertTrue of a dot equals b, which prints expected true, was false.

66
00:04:54,547 --> 00:04:57,617
Use assertEquals, which prints both values.

67
00:04:57,617 --> 00:05:00,330
Never disable a test without a reason.

68
00:05:00,330 --> 00:05:03,328
Never put loops or conditionals in a test.

69
00:05:03,328 --> 00:05:07,111
And never depend on the system clock: inject a Clock.

70
00:05:08,011 --> 00:05:08,644
To recap.

71
00:05:08,644 --> 00:05:15,321
The Platform launches tests, Jupiter is what you write, and Vintage lets you migrate gradually.

72
00:05:15,321 --> 00:05:16,656
Extensions compose.

73
00:05:16,656 --> 00:05:22,841
Use assertThrows with a message check, and assertAll for several parts of one behaviour.

74
00:05:22,841 --> 00:05:27,339
Parameterize the boundaries, and use nested classes for context.

75
00:05:27,789 --> 00:05:31,256
Next is chapter forty-two: Mockito and test doubles.

76
00:05:31,256 --> 00:05:32,589
Thanks for watching.
